WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.261 --> 00:00:01.403
Kevin.

00:00:03.625 --> 00:00:07.950
They found an air hostess
who is up for carrying drugs.

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:09.480
This does happen.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:10.650
When I was in prison in Ecuador,

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:12.930
they arrested the captain of an airline.

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:15.880
My name's Pieter Tritton, aka Posh Pete.

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.995
I am a former drug
trafficker, namely cocaine,

00:00:18.995 --> 00:00:22.560
from beginning of 2003
till the end of 2005.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:26.376
I served just over 10 years'
imprisonment in Ecuador.

00:00:26.376 --> 00:00:29.330
Today, we will be
watching clips from movies

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:31.050
and TVs about drug trafficking,

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:32.934
judge how realistic they are.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.267
The one thing I see repeated in film

00:00:40.267 --> 00:00:42.390
is that there's this scene of, you know,

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:44.310
loads of women naked

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.080
so that they supposedly
can't steal the drugs

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.810
in a lab somewhere with loads of chemicals

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:50.033
and loads of drugs.

00:00:50.033 --> 00:00:51.960
It's just unrealistic, in my opinion.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.210
The women in this scene are
wearing masks and gloves.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:56.130
It's likely in a lab that you would have

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:57.990
that sort of protective equipment on,

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:00.512
'cause I mean, I've been in makeshift labs

00:01:00.512 --> 00:01:01.560
when we were doing our thing,

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.420
and the amount of dust and fumes

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.753
and everything that gets up
into the air and onto hands,

00:01:05.753 --> 00:01:06.993
the chemicals are really toxic.

00:01:06.993 --> 00:01:09.202
Álvarez: Let me show you distribution.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:13.045
Jax: How many dealers?

00:01:13.045 --> 00:01:14.946
Álvarez: 28. Looking to triple that.

00:01:14.946 --> 00:01:17.582
They've got about 48 people in this scene,

00:01:17.582 --> 00:01:18.960
let alone the dealers.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.402
Having that amount of drugs
in one place at one time

00:01:21.402 --> 00:01:23.915
with that amount of
people, it's just such,

00:01:23.915 --> 00:01:25.380
it would be such high risk.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:26.807
All the different parts of the operation

00:01:26.807 --> 00:01:28.715
are all in one place. Never.

00:01:28.715 --> 00:01:30.883
Each individual mechanism or part

00:01:30.883 --> 00:01:32.190
would be separate to the other,

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:34.320
so the people that had the safe house,

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.270
they wouldn't know people
that were in the lab.

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:38.910
The people in the lab
wouldn't know the drivers.

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:41.550
So if any one part got hit by the police,

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:43.563
they couldn't be linked to the others.

00:01:43.563 --> 00:01:45.185
I would give this a 5 just because

00:01:45.185 --> 00:01:47.504
it's all just too much in one place.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:55.175
Steve: Before long, we were
seizing 60 kilos of coke a day.

00:01:55.175 --> 00:01:56.880
That could happen.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.190
I mean, there was an
instance a few years ago

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:01.230
in Spain where boxes of bananas

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:02.910
were literally getting delivered to shops,

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:04.680
and they were taking out
the top layer of bananas

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:06.480
and the rest was full of cocaine.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:08.130
The flower industry in South America,

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:09.660
particularly Colombia, is huge.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:11.940
So any cover good, as we call it.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:15.150
But generally cartels don't
send large amounts like this

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:18.117
unless they've got customs
that are either paid off

00:02:18.117 --> 00:02:19.920
or they know they've got a guaranteed in.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.710
For the authorities to
get big hits like this,

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:24.480
it means something's
probably gone wrong somewhere

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.280
along the line, obviously,

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:27.949
for the cartel, that is.

00:02:27.949 --> 00:02:30.990
Steve: We felt we were
making a huge difference.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:33.600
If you look at the name on
the boat, it's Guayaquil,

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:35.730
and that's the city that I
was in prison in in Ecuador.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:37.800
That's the main drug-trafficking route

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.358
out of Ecuador, up to Central America,

00:02:40.358 --> 00:02:41.610
and then on into America.

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:43.230
I was in prison with several people

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:45.990
who were working on the boats.

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:47.440
They were captains of boats.

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:53.040
Steve: Truth is, we
weren't even making a dent.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.310
In that tube, I mean,
they tended to fit cocaine

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:58.403
to the shape of whatever it was packed in

00:02:58.403 --> 00:03:01.354
in order to maximize the
amount you can get in there.

00:03:01.354 --> 00:03:03.630
Kilos are normally wrapped
with a rubber skin.

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:06.210
There's normally about
six or seven layers.

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:09.510
Steve: They let us have 60
so they could bring in 600.

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:11.220
Whether or not that's true, I don't know.

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:14.520
The cartel's giving up drugs
so that they can bring in more.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:16.320
Maybe they do. I've been
in prison with people

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:18.090
from the Sinaloa Cartel,

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:20.281
and I've never heard them
say that they did that.

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:30.120
The progression of the process

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:34.500
of the making of the cocaine
here is very realistic.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:35.790
That's the base product

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:38.400
from when the leaves are crushed down

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:39.570
and soaked in kerosene.

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:42.030
They then go through a few more stages.

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:44.730
They're put into trays
to dry it, basically.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:47.370
I mean, they have missed
out a couple of stages.

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:48.540
I mean, as they would.

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:51.431
They don't tell everybody
exactly how to make it.

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:56.193
This scene is very realistic,

00:03:56.193 --> 00:03:59.435
where they've got the sort
of temporary camp setup

00:03:59.435 --> 00:04:02.343
out in the jungle somewhere,
processing the leaves.

00:04:02.343 --> 00:04:04.380
I mean, the volumes of
chemicals that they're using,

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:06.272
in South America, they have the chemicals

00:04:06.272 --> 00:04:08.495
highly controlled now.
So it's very difficult

00:04:08.495 --> 00:04:09.990
for them to buy the
volumes that they need.

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:12.150
For 1 kilo of cocaine with ether,

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:14.880
you need at least 6 or 7 liters per kilo.

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:16.439
So you imagine, if
they're making, you know,

00:04:16.439 --> 00:04:18.527
300, 400 kilos a day,

00:04:18.527 --> 00:04:20.910
you're talking about
tank loads of chemicals.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:30.898
I doubt you would be given access

00:04:30.898 --> 00:04:32.314
to a laboratory like that.

00:04:32.314 --> 00:04:35.250
If you've given away its
location of where it was,

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:38.130
you'd be revealing the whole
makeup just for a kilo,

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:40.350
when they're producing 300 kilos a day.

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:42.390
If you're talking to a guy
that's running a lab like this,

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:43.223
you're going to be buying,

00:04:43.223 --> 00:04:45.240
I mean, as a minimum 50 or 100.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:48.330
The way that they're dressed
in these scenes is typical

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:50.130
because these guys working in the labs,

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:51.450
they're out in the middle of a jungle.

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:53.760
You're not going to be showing
off to anybody in there.

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:55.680
The actual workers are very poorly paid.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:58.922
You know, it's bad
conditions. It's dangerous.

00:04:58.922 --> 00:05:00.462
You've got the Colombian military

00:05:00.462 --> 00:05:01.860
looking for you all the time.

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:03.482
I mean, you can see that guy there

00:05:03.482 --> 00:05:05.790
is sort of dressed half
in military fatigues.

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:06.720
That's quite common.

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:08.910
And often they'll be
dressed in rubber boots

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:11.702
like Wellingtons, just 'cause
of the conditions underfoot.

00:05:11.702 --> 00:05:12.720
Yeah, around 8.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:13.861
They've done their research.

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:23.447
I was in prison, again,

00:05:23.447 --> 00:05:26.423
with a guy from the
Sinaloa Cartel, a pilot.

00:05:26.423 --> 00:05:28.140
He used to fly Cessnas just like this.

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:29.625
And he told me stories about literally

00:05:29.625 --> 00:05:33.570
coming down jungle
airstrips like this, dirt,

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:35.640
in a Cessna with a ton and a half,

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:37.050
2 tons of cocaine in the back.

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:38.490
Barry: You get another load of powder,

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:39.540
take it back to the States

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:42.150
with a quick stop to refuel in Panama

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:45.390
under the protection of my
old friend Col. Noriega.

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:47.340
They were paying off Noriega.

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:49.800
It's been proven that he was
involved in drug trafficking.

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:52.401
I mean, you imagine the
amount of money involved

00:05:52.401 --> 00:05:55.200
in all this trafficking
that's washing around.

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:56.730
There's so much corruption.

00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:59.347
They would try and pay
people like Noriega.

00:05:59.347 --> 00:06:01.080
Barry: I had to get some help.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:03.870
I called these boys my snowbirds.

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:05.898
They were using multiple planes.

00:06:05.898 --> 00:06:08.603
So, they would probably
limit the amount of times

00:06:08.603 --> 00:06:10.798
they used a certain plane in a month,

00:06:10.798 --> 00:06:12.480
or however frequently,

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:14.280
in order to try and avoid detection.

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:17.190
I mean, certainly in America
they would have records,

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:19.080
you know, flight plans
and stuff like that.

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:20.040
We did things like that.

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:21.750
When we were using
passengers, for example,

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:23.160
we wouldn't use them any more than one

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:24.618
or two times in a month.

00:06:30.755 --> 00:06:32.310
I know people that have
done this into Britain

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:34.140
using light aircraft coming from Holland,

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:36.210
predestined drop zone.

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:38.190
You just drop it, and then
you have people waiting

00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:40.020
in that area to collect,

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:41.820
or you have, like, a private runway,

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:43.410
and if you unload it quick enough,

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:45.785
you make sure that you've
got the place secure,

00:06:45.785 --> 00:06:48.785
there's no police, just
fire it out the plane

00:06:48.785 --> 00:06:50.190
as quick as you can and get out of there.

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:51.470
It is quite high risk.

00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:52.950
I mean, that's still happening, for sure.

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:55.966
Particularly in Central
America and South America.

00:06:55.966 --> 00:06:57.510
Maybe not into America these days

00:06:57.510 --> 00:06:59.880
because the security's that good.

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:01.019
7.

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:10.170
So, this is making the
capsules in order to swallow.

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:11.460
I've seen them made like this.

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:14.940
So, this is a press to
just compress the cocaine

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:16.290
in order to make the capsule.

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:18.390
In this instance, they're
wrapping them in condoms,

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:20.280
which is quite often how they're made.

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:23.880
I would say very high-risk
way of trafficking cocaine.

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:26.580
And depending on the person's stomach,

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:30.810
generally, you are talking
about 800 grams to 1,200 grams,

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:33.502
so around a kilo that they'll swallow.

00:07:41.316 --> 00:07:42.424
Yuck.

00:07:42.424 --> 00:07:45.120
I never sent people this way.

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:49.480
A, I didn't want somebody
ending up dead on my account,

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:52.630
and B, it was quite
risky. Well, highly risky.

00:07:52.630 --> 00:07:55.787
They all tell you that the
capsules are 100% safe,

00:07:55.787 --> 00:07:57.060
there's no way they can split,

00:07:57.060 --> 00:07:59.220
all sorts of stuff to
get you to swallow them.

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:00.900
Whereas in reality, you know,

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:03.450
you stand a high chance of them bursting.

00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:05.190
When I was in Ecuador, it was in the news.

00:08:05.190 --> 00:08:08.397
A couple of people had swallowed
capsules that had burst,

00:08:08.397 --> 00:08:09.660
and they'd been found dead.

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:11.670
Drug trafficking can be portrayed

00:08:11.670 --> 00:08:12.930
as being glamorous a lot,

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:15.039
but it is not very glamorous at all.

00:08:15.039 --> 00:08:16.397
It's a pretty gruesome trade.

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:25.770
I mean, these are typical questions.

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:28.320
They would ask to see, for example,

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:30.233
"Have you bought your own
ticket?" because if you haven't,

00:08:30.233 --> 00:08:33.838
if somebody else has bought
it for you, then why?

00:08:33.838 --> 00:08:36.510
That's a big red flag.

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:38.340
So she's just trying to catch her out here

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:40.650
and see how much it
costs and if she knows.

00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:41.816
If she doesn't, then obviously

00:08:41.816 --> 00:08:43.024
there's something wrong, probably.

00:08:43.024 --> 00:08:44.742
Which is quite unlikely in Colombia,

00:08:44.742 --> 00:08:47.392
as, you know, $800 is lot of money.

00:08:57.834 --> 00:09:00.171
The ways of proving it, obviously, is

00:09:00.171 --> 00:09:03.390
law enforcement will stop
the passenger and X-ray them,

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:06.641
or nowadays use the scanner as well,

00:09:06.641 --> 00:09:08.640
which is a much more detailed picture

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:09.840
of what's going on inside.

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:12.630
It's definitely a lot harder
to use this method these days.

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:14.550
Another thing they'll do is
they'll put you in a room

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:16.860
if they really think you've
definitely got drugs in you

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:20.700
until you go to the toilet.
And there's a collection pan,

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:23.042
so anything that goes
through there gets collected.

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:31.208
This is the sort of thing that happens.

00:09:31.208 --> 00:09:34.745
I got stopped on the
first job that we did.

00:09:34.745 --> 00:09:36.510
I brought the tent back myself,

00:09:36.510 --> 00:09:40.440
which contained 5 kilos of
cocaine impregnated in rubber.

00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:42.000
I was over the luggage allowance,

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:44.040
so I had to give loads of
stuff away in the airport

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:46.560
but kept ahold of this
great big 10-man tent.

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:48.977
Got stopped in Amsterdam
at Schiphol Airport,

00:09:48.977 --> 00:09:51.129
questioned, and released

00:09:51.129 --> 00:09:53.700
and let through carrying
5 kilos of cocaine.

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:55.020
I'd give that 7 or 8.

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:56.550
How much she was swallowing,

00:09:56.550 --> 00:09:58.620
the kind of questioning they undertook.

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:01.475
I've gone into schools
and just spoken about

00:10:01.475 --> 00:10:03.810
what happened to me and
how I became involved in it

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:06.843
and how bad it was in order
to try and prevent them

00:10:06.843 --> 00:10:09.240
from becoming involved themselves.

00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:20.791
Tunnels have been documented.

00:10:20.791 --> 00:10:23.190
It's a known method that
they've used in the past.

00:10:23.190 --> 00:10:25.410
They just want to get it
out and get it dispersed.

00:10:25.410 --> 00:10:26.910
I mean, that's the most dangerous point,

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:28.290
when it's coming into the country.

00:10:28.290 --> 00:10:30.330
So they just want to
get it out of that area

00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:31.830
and somewhere safe, to the safe house,

00:10:31.830 --> 00:10:33.147
as quick as possible, really.

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:43.020
Chapo Guzmán was the head
of the Sinaloa Cartel.

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:43.853
He's a real person.

00:10:43.853 --> 00:10:46.530
He's in prison in America
somewhere for life.

00:10:46.530 --> 00:10:48.270
And these guys do oversee things.

00:10:48.270 --> 00:10:50.430
The amount of trust in
people that you need

00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:53.190
to be shipping tons of
cocaine around the world.

00:10:53.190 --> 00:10:56.480
That's one thing in films
that's not quite true,

00:10:56.480 --> 00:10:59.640
is this idea of the supercriminal,

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:01.081
they're just on a phone

00:11:01.081 --> 00:11:02.937
and a laptop and everything happens,

00:11:02.937 --> 00:11:04.308
and they have all these
people working for them,

00:11:04.308 --> 00:11:06.750
and they never go near anything, ever.

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:08.160
Yeah, I'd give this 7 out of 10.

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:09.270
Derek: Seems really risky.

00:11:09.270 --> 00:11:11.850
Derek, she's a stewardess.
They don't check her bags.

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:12.683
Kevin.

00:11:12.683 --> 00:11:14.610
They've found a weakness in a system.

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:19.170
They've found an air hostess
who is up for carrying drugs.

00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:20.700
This does happen.

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:22.110
When I was in prison in Ecuador,

00:11:22.110 --> 00:11:24.120
they arrested the captain of an airline

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:25.080
on several occasions.

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:26.520
There were people involved

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:28.050
with the airlines that had been arrested.

00:11:28.050 --> 00:11:29.550
This is back in the day, isn't it?

00:11:29.550 --> 00:11:31.200
I've met old-school drug traffickers

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:33.690
where it was literally,
put drugs in a suitcase

00:11:33.690 --> 00:11:35.040
and get on a plane and traffic them,

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:38.550
because the border controls
was so lax back then.

00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:40.710
But these days, and
particularly after 9/11,

00:11:40.710 --> 00:11:43.200
the border controls and the security

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:45.150
around airlines is so high.

00:11:45.150 --> 00:11:48.810
Getting drugs through passenger
aircraft is very difficult.

00:11:48.810 --> 00:11:50.340
Pure coke, it melts away

00:11:50.340 --> 00:11:54.360
at about 185, 190 degrees.

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:58.243
Cutting agents, they
melt away at about 100.

00:11:58.243 --> 00:11:59.940
They're looking to see if
the drugs have been cut

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:01.530
or adulterated at this stage.

00:12:01.530 --> 00:12:05.372
This is 100% a realistic
method of testing cocaine.

00:12:05.372 --> 00:12:07.650
We used to use a piece of cast iron

00:12:07.650 --> 00:12:09.177
with a thermometer set into it,

00:12:09.177 --> 00:12:11.820
and you put the cocaine
on top of it, on the iron,

00:12:11.820 --> 00:12:13.078
put that on top of a cooker,

00:12:13.078 --> 00:12:15.153
and different chemicals in the cocaine

00:12:15.153 --> 00:12:16.470
burn off at different temperatures.

00:12:16.470 --> 00:12:17.820
And as you hear in the film,

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:20.520
cocaine in its purest form starts to burn

00:12:20.520 --> 00:12:23.010
or evaporate at about 180, I think it is.

00:12:23.010 --> 00:12:26.010
I know a lot of cocaine is
getting adulterated these days

00:12:26.010 --> 00:12:27.578
with things like boric acid,

00:12:27.578 --> 00:12:30.049
levamisole, which I think is a pig wormer.

00:12:30.049 --> 00:12:31.380
There's a multitude of things

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:33.090
that they use to cut the drugs with.

00:12:33.090 --> 00:12:35.520
These cutting agents are
all pretty toxic, I suppose.

00:12:35.520 --> 00:12:38.640
So, I mean, I wouldn't say
taking any drug is safe,

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:40.170
but the more it's cut, the worse it gets.

00:12:40.170 --> 00:12:43.353
Fairly realistic. I'd say 7 or 8.

00:12:51.690 --> 00:12:54.540
The quantities involved
in this clip are huge.

00:12:54.540 --> 00:12:56.160
How realistic that is, I don't know,

00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:58.275
because I wasn't involved
with methamphetamine.

00:12:58.275 --> 00:13:00.666
But to me, the amount
of people that are there

00:13:00.666 --> 00:13:01.753
and the scale,

00:13:01.753 --> 00:13:04.140
in America, I doubt it very much.

00:13:04.140 --> 00:13:06.990
Normally, there's nearly always a hiccup.

00:13:06.990 --> 00:13:09.559
Things get delayed, there's a problem,

00:13:09.559 --> 00:13:11.842
someone's f---ed up.

00:13:11.842 --> 00:13:14.523
There's just always, nearly
always some sort of problem.

00:13:18.540 --> 00:13:22.590
That's just to identify which
containers have the drugs in.

00:13:22.590 --> 00:13:23.970
So they're using the UV stamp,

00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:25.860
which is obviously not
visible to the naked eye,

00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:28.715
but as soon as you put a UV light on it

00:13:28.715 --> 00:13:31.260
they're going to know which
containers have the drugs in.

00:13:31.260 --> 00:13:34.560
Putting UV stamps on containers is one way

00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:36.469
of identifying where the drugs are.

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:45.260
Normally, in any serious
drug-trafficking operation,

00:13:45.260 --> 00:13:47.340
you're going to have front businesses

00:13:47.340 --> 00:13:49.650
to facilitate the movement of the drugs

00:13:49.650 --> 00:13:52.084
or the inflow of the drugs or the outflow

00:13:52.084 --> 00:13:55.050
or the laundering of money as well.

00:13:55.050 --> 00:13:58.238
Companies are quite often vital

00:13:58.238 --> 00:14:02.070
to drug-trafficking networks
for numerous reasons.

00:14:02.070 --> 00:14:03.570
I'd give that probably a 5.

00:14:03.570 --> 00:14:06.420
Just the amount of people
involved and the scale.

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:16.200
Hey, you're going to drive to San Diego.

00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:18.182
So, yeah, this is quite a common method

00:14:18.182 --> 00:14:19.440
of moving drugs around.

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:21.460
Putting drugs into a caleta,

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:23.640
or a hiding place, in the vehicle,

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:25.860
which they will have manufactured or made.

00:14:25.860 --> 00:14:28.590
Certain vehicles have
particularly good hiding places,

00:14:28.590 --> 00:14:32.507
voids and spaces that can
be utilized as hiding spots.

00:14:39.570 --> 00:14:40.950
I mean, the British police have got these.

00:14:40.950 --> 00:14:42.270
It's a mirror on a handle,

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:44.150
so they're looking underneath the car

00:14:44.150 --> 00:14:46.230
to see if there's any new welding spots,

00:14:46.230 --> 00:14:49.200
anything obvious, or
anything that's out of place

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:51.510
that might show a hiding spot.

00:14:51.510 --> 00:14:53.135
Obviously, they've got
a sniffer dog there.

00:14:55.170 --> 00:14:56.940
I mean, if you're that far in the queue,

00:14:56.940 --> 00:14:59.322
I mean, you can't really run
away from it, then, can you?

00:14:59.322 --> 00:15:00.450
As soon as you get out of the vehicle,

00:15:00.450 --> 00:15:02.070
they're going to spot you, apprehend you.

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:03.180
This is the first one she's done,

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:04.830
but, I mean, they would
probably be using people

00:15:04.830 --> 00:15:06.660
that they'd already used before anyway

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:08.850
that they knew were trustworthy and solid.

00:15:08.850 --> 00:15:10.673
When we were sending people, we, you know,

00:15:10.673 --> 00:15:12.810
the reason I did that first one myself

00:15:12.810 --> 00:15:13.890
was because I didn't want to send people

00:15:13.890 --> 00:15:15.120
to do something that I hadn't.

00:15:15.120 --> 00:15:19.842
So I would be able to know
what to tell them to expect.

00:15:19.842 --> 00:15:21.270
Quite often I would go,

00:15:21.270 --> 00:15:22.710
I'd be in the background somewhere,

00:15:22.710 --> 00:15:24.480
just to make sure they were all right.

00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:25.980
This is quite realistic.

00:15:25.980 --> 00:15:27.870
Give that probably 6,

00:15:27.870 --> 00:15:29.790
because it's fairly realistic.

00:15:29.790 --> 00:15:32.550
I myself have become kind
of a little bit bored

00:15:32.550 --> 00:15:34.440
of watching films about drug trafficking,

00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:35.550
having lived it.

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:39.570
I mean, I've witnessed so
many people getting killed

00:15:39.570 --> 00:15:42.900
in the most horrific means
and ways that you can imagine,

00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:46.680
all because of the money that's involved.

00:15:46.680 --> 00:15:48.690
So, I mean, if you do
think it's glamorous,

00:15:48.690 --> 00:15:50.310
then I don't know what life you're living,

00:15:50.310 --> 00:15:52.410
but it's not the one that
I lived, that's for sure.

00:15:52.410 --> 00:15:55.020
So, thanks for watching the clips today.

00:15:55.020 --> 00:15:56.978
If you want to see more of the same,

00:15:56.978 --> 00:15:58.544
